# Agent routing

This bundle is a verified local execution harness. It supplies capability; the current user and target repository remain authoritative for intent, dependencies, commands, safety, and architecture.

1. Read the target repository's applicable `AGENTS.md` and supported command surface first. They define **what** work is permitted and how the project expects it to be invoked.
2. For local execution that this bundle supports, prefer its verified capability over ad-hoc host installs, unpinned global substitutes, or remote/connector workarounds. A target repository's own pinned tool or command remains authoritative when it deliberately owns that capability.
3. Use `agent-env capabilities` to discover the bundled surface, `agent-env help` for syntax, and `agent-env doctor` when host compatibility or an external capability is uncertain. Do not preload the full README just to discover tools.
4. Use the local harness for design, analysis, build, configuration, and testing when it can perform the work. Use platform connectors/apps for external capabilities the bundle cannot supply or the host blocks—for example remote GitHub when shell networking is unavailable—not as a substitute for supported local verification.
5. PostgreSQL is explicit capability, not ambient PATH. Use repository-owned database commands when defined; otherwise `agent-env postgres run ...` creates an isolated disposable local server and `agent-env pg TOOL ...` invokes a bundled client utility.
6. `agent-env pg-delta plan` is plan-only and accepts only numeric-loopback live database targets. Upstream `apply`/`sync` are not exposed.
7. Offline Node capsules never create project dependencies. `agent-env node-deps hydrate` acts only when the repository lock exactly matches a bundled package/version/integrity.
8. If shell GitHub is genuinely useful, run `agent-env github` once; use `agent-env github-git` only when the current HTTPS worktree actually needs Git credential wiring. Do not repeatedly retry known-blocked shell networking. When a platform connector supplies a standard Git handoff ZIP, use `agent-env git-handoff restore ARTIFACT.zip DEST` to reconstruct a verified local worktree; otherwise keep remote operations in the connector.
9. Credentials remain host/session state. The verified payload is immutable; caches, ad-hoc tools, disposable databases, and other mutable data belong under `state/`.
10. Preserve one logical long-running validation. Give it an adequate outer timeout, or keep/supervise and poll that same process. Do not fragment a suite merely to satisfy an agent wrapper timeout.
11. If target work needs a reusable capability this bundle does not provide and the shared execution layer is the correct owner, make the gap explicit and request/raise a bounded issue against `CrownOpsEng/magnet-photos-env` rather than normalizing a persistent workaround. Do not hide project dependencies in the toolbox.
12. Read the runtime README only for deeper operating, recovery, provenance, or capability detail.
